          %title
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Maharam’s theorem states a complete classification of isomorphism classes of the appropriate
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+measure+theory"} category of measurable spaces
          %p
            In the case of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-finite+measure+spaces"} σ-finite measure spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with measurable inverse such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}
            preserve measure 0 sets.
          %p
            As explained in the article
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+measure+theory"} categories of measure theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-finite+measure+spaces"} σ-finite measure spaces
            there are additional subtleties to consider: equality almost everywhere must be refined to weak equality almost everywhere, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-finite+measure+spaces"} σ-finite measure spaces
            should be replaced by Marczewski-compact strictly localizible measure spaces. For the sake of brevity, we refer to such objects simply as
            %strong measure spaces
            below. (As explained in detail in the article
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+measure+theory"} categories of measure theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-ideal"} σ-ideal
            of sets of measure 0.)
          %p
            In this unrestricted form, by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand-type+duality+for+commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} Gelfand-type duality for commutative von Neumann algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+Boolean+algebras"} localizable Boolean algebras
            (equivalently:
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locales"} measurable locales
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+von+Neumann+algebras"} abelian von Neumann algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+spaces"} hyperstonean spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+locales"} hyperstonean locales
          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            Every measure space canonically decomposes as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            (disjoint union) of ergodic measure spaces. Here a measure space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %strong ergodic
            if the only subobjects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e., equivalence classes of measurable subsets modulo measure 0 subsets) invariant under all automorphisms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself.
          %p
            Furthermore, an ergodic measure space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is (noncanically, using the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔠
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}\times 2^\kappa
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is 0 or infinite, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}
            is infinite if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is infinite. Here the cardinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}
            is known as the
            %em cellularity
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is its
            = succeed "." do
              %em Maharam type
          %p
            In particular, if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa=0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}
            of their set of atoms.
          %p
            Otherwise,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is infinite, and we get a classification of isomorphism classes of ergodic atomless (alias diffuse) measure spaces: such spaces are isomorphic to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔠
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}\times 2^\kappa
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            are infinite cardinals.
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa=\aleph_0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi κ
                    %mo ≅
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\kappa\cong\mathbf{R}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa\gt\aleph_0
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-finite+measure+spaces"} σ-finite measure spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+processes"} stochastic processes
          %p
            Thus, a completely general object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi κ
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔠
                    %mi κ
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi κ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\sqcup\coprod_\kappa \mathfrak{c}_\kappa\times 2^\kappa,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a discrete measure space,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            runs over all infinite cardinals,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔠
                    %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}_\kappa
            is an infinite cardinal or 0, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔠
                    %mi κ
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{c}_\kappa\ne0
            only for a set of cardinals
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
