          %h2#relative_version Relative version
          %p There is also a relative version of Maharam’s theorem, which classifies morphisms in any of the equivalent categories considered above.
          %p
            Observe that morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi κ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\kappa\to2^\lambda
            exist if and only if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo ≥
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa\ge\lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^0
            (i.e., a singleton) to the real line
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}\cong 2^{\aleph_0}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} commutative von Neumann algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
