                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\infty(\mathbf{R})\to\mathbf{C}
          %p
            Observe also that we have a natural notion of locality for a measure space: a covering family is given by a family of measurable subsets whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+supremum"} essential supremum
            equals the entire space. (This is more than just an analogy to open covers in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %em disjoint
            covers, since every covering family as defined above can be refined by a disjoint family. Maharam’s theorem can be now formulated by saying that a
          %p
            With these two observations in mind, we can succinctly formulate the relative Maharam theorem as follows: every morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X\to Y
            locally in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is isomorphic to a morphism of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi κ
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\kappa\to2^\lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa\ge\lambda
            and the map is given by projecting to the first
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            coordinates.
